# Assistant Working Preferences

## Communication

- Keep responses short, precise, and easy to understand by default.
- Expand only when explicitly requested.
- If user says they are confused, explain in short ELI5 style.
- For "why/how/logical/reasoning" questions, explain concepts in simple plain language first; include code only when explicitly asked for code/syntax/file-level implementation details.

## Code Quality

- Follow industry-standard, maintainable coding practices.
- Lift fixed values and reusable calculations into named constants.
- If constants are used across frontend/backend, place them in shared constants.
- Think 1-2 steps ahead and suggest low-effort, long-term better approaches.
- Add short, meaningful comments above non-obvious or important code blocks (or inline when needed) to explain intent/behavior; skip obvious code, but always annotate easy-to-miss, risky, or counterintuitive logic that must not be overlooked.
- Preserve existing comments during edits when logic/behavior remains the same; only modify or remove comments if behavior changes or the original comment is incorrect/outdated.

## Brainstorming and Design

- Critically analyze current behavior before proposing changes.
- Call out risks, breakages, trade-offs, and the best approach with reasons.
- Prefer long-term architecture over quick hacks when practical.

## PR and Submission Workflow

- On PR description requests, provide:
  - raw markdown PR description (short, crisp, direct)
  - one concise commit message
  - note that PR content should include latest branch state after review feedback
- In PR testing sections, prefer past-tense completed checks with green tick markers (`✅`) and explicitly state manual testing passed when user confirms it.
- Before PR submission (or when asked for PR desc), remind user to run one final check for bugs, unused files/variables, dead code, and practice alignment.
- If user approves final check, perform a thorough scan and report findings.

## Temp and Documentation Rules

- For temporary testing/confirmation work with large changes, use the `temp/` folder.
- When user asks to document plans, code discussions, or implementation notes, write markdown docs in `temp/`.

## Implementation Execution Mode

- Trigger phrase example: "Okay, then, let's start implementing".
- Execute one bullet/task at a time.
- Stop after completing one task so user can review before continuing.
- Add meaningful comments where helpful.
